ng profit-making  machines basically  some of these are even private gated  cities  it's almost like a gigantic mall except  it's the size of a city  there are other private cities that are  listed on stock exchanges and they're  answerable to shareholders  and so a tremendous amount is being  invested in these projects in the hopes  that they'll attract foreign direct  investment and sort of spark a new type  of digital economy  one of the most expensive examples of  this idea is songdo international  business district  the city is built on reclaimed land in  the yellow sea just a two-hour flight  from major destinations in asia  the land reclamation began in the  mid-1990s and songdo became an extension  of incheon a neighbouring city whose  government set up the incheon free  economic zone to facilitate free trade  between south korea and the rest of the  world including china which at that  point wasn't a part of the world trade  organization  so i think they might have purposely not  to make you feel like it's a korean city  the incheon government contracted kpf a  new york-based architecture firm to  design songdo's master plan  lead designer james von klemperer and  over 60 architects spent eight years  completing the project  there were hundreds of consultants of  engineers  designers forecasters  material science people  so overall you probably had i would say  up to a thousand  different designers and consultants  working on this city at one time  [Music]  we don't make a city without any kind of  templates and any kind of precedence  that inform us about urban life as they  say we stand on the shoulders of others  and rather than trying to start with  some new theory about human interaction  and  building density and form and urban  patterns we borrowed in a way  uh pieces of solutions from other cities  so we sort of pulled and pushed and like  like an artist making a collage with  different preset elements  try to use these pieces of cities  as inspirations and borrow from some of  their dimensioning systems and  strategies  these ideas were all woven into songdo's  urban fabric the physical structure of  every city that defines its character  things like building types open spaces  roads and lighting  the team created neighborhoods using a  pattern of streets  local stores  and a mix of low-rise buildings and  towers  all centred around a school and parks  [Music]  when it comes to the urban fabric of  sundo we have a relatively large city  block and large avenues and a lot of  really big scale green areas and open  spaces  alberto gonzalez is an architect and  urban planner and has been living in  songdo since 2017  one of the nice things about sunder  urban design is that the blocks are very  open so you can actually walk through  all the blocks many of them have outdoor  gyms others have playgrounds for kids so  it's literally open for anyone who wants  to go in  and that's a kind of a key distinction  if you compare to other  asian cities in which you typically have  gated communities  so yeah here is uh one of the small  pocket parks and um even though you are  not too far from one of the main avenues  you feel like uh in a more protected  side i think it really makes a city a  bit more vibrant  one of the tenets  that we accepted or embraced in in new  songdo city plan was to make it a  pedestrian city  a place where you'd be comfortable  walking from district to district or  within districts but korean planning and  korean practice of building had embraced  the automobile those who were working  with us from  government ministries said you must have  an eight-lane road at the edge of this  part of town  or even going through the middle of a  neighborhood we said well  that doesn't sound very  attractive  kpf couldn't stop the highways being  built so instead they focused on how  they were designed  zoning restrictions in korea mean that  buildings along these large avenues are  usually set back from the street to  protect residents from noise and  pollution  but the large sidewalks created as a  result often lack character like  textures on the facades or shops and  cafes that make streets interesting for  people to use  and so we had to in essence  break or change the korean code to be  able to have uh architectural textures  and buildings right on the street  it would have been very difficult to do  with a small one-off design but the kind  of  scale of this project warranted that  everybody stepped back and think again  about what made  uh the best kind of rules for building  a  transportation will get better but until  then i'm going to use my car  some elements of songdo's design like  reducing car use may not be fully  realized yet but with forty percent of  the city left to be built there's a call  out for initiatives from the public on  how to improve it  alberto is consulting on one of those  projects  we are exploring uh ways in which we can  bring the space back to the people  reduce the usage of cars  so this is one of the spots that people  criticize a lot and of course with a lot  of reasons we have like a total of ten  lanes five on each side plus the buffer  area i mean we literally have a huge  avenue it's almost like a highway type  of scale so the idea is that in this  central section there's enough room to  put in a light rail here like a tram and  maybe you know one or two lanes that  both sides can get reduced and can be  occupied with sitting areas with pop-up  kiosks  so by getting rid of certain areas that  are now purely used by cars  we can actually increase the chances for  you know randomness to happen so uh this  type of urban randomness is actually  what makes city life much more  interesting  technology was another key element in  songdo's initial design and it became  one of the first smart city projects in  the world  i see a lot of cctv if you go  out on the street you don't see any  power poles or wires they're all  underground  cisco committed to spending 47 million  to provide the city with smart  infrastructure  we designed systems  that would ensure  a highly efficient use of energy and  other resources of water even the use of  waste so this is the sort of thing you  can do when you design a city from  scratch  because those infrastructural systems  are not there  this is songdo's operation center which  manages all the infrastructure as well  as monitors over 5000 sensors and  cameras spread around the city  foreign  a lot of these new city projects brand  themselves as smart  they are trying to be the silicon valley  of southeast asia or the silicon valley  of latin america  or the silicon valley of africa etc  they seem to be inspired by this sort of  entrepreneurial stance of taking big  risks moving fast and breaking things  but it's not just their money that  they're risking they're risking land  they're risking the lives of residents  who relocate to these places  the risk of failure is high with some  new cities turning into expensive ghost  towns that aren't designed to cater to  the needs of locals  oftentimes locals are priced out of  these new cities because the new cities  are not built for them they're built for  investors so we're seeing uh cities that  are only 10  20 occupied the units are all sold  there's just nobody living in them  there are a lot of ghost cities in china  that have been filled in there are other  ghost cities that are still very  under-occupied  but whether these new cities get built  or not whether they happen or not  whether they fill in or not people are  still going to make money  songdo seems to be defying this ghost  city trend its population is tracking  its development now at about 60 percent  of the original target of 300 000 and  there's an appetite to replicate its  success  the development has been described as an  example of a city in a box with  ready-made and tested solutions that can  be quickly applied elsewhere  the same developer and architects were  contracted to design a new city in china  called mei shi lake its detailed master  plan took two and a half years while  songdoze took four  no city should ever  be a copy of another city  because uh  the the particular needs of place and  time are so different  but  there are some techniques in city  building that have been taken from one  city to another quite successfully  and you could see if somebody needs to  build very quickly  they might want to understand some  templates  of techniques that have been used before  i think a song though is a successful  story it's definitely something that is  going to be a work in progress  and the typical question is can we  replicate this  can this city in a box type of  environment be literally transferred to  other areas  i think there's so many ingredients that  came  into the  mix  for songdo to happen that i certainly  think that it's  not easy to replicate  this sort of notion that we can build  something  that other people will want to emulate  and  will be the first will be a model for  other people to copy that's a really  appealing  aspect of a lot of these new cities  cities evolve over the centuries and  over the millennia into  you know cities that we know today  beijing or delhi or other major  historical cities  and so given time maybe any new city can  fill in and evolve and become something  lively and successful  [Music]  you

